  "live_records": {
    "q-128093": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128093",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $a=⟨5,-8,-7⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,1,2⟩,$ and $c=⟨-3,1,0⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i-6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨5,-8,-7⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,-6,2⟩ \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)+(-8)\\cdot (-6)+(-7)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
