    "q-20065": {
      "dom_id": "step-q20065",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $a=⟨0,-2,3⟩,$ $b=⟨4,-2,1⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,-1,2⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))i-(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)j+(4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3i-5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨0,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
