    "q-317325": {
      "dom_id": "step-q317325",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Let $a=⟨-1,-2,3⟩$, $b=⟨4,-1,-2⟩$ and $c=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\,((-1)(-3)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\,-\\,(-2)\\,(4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\,+\\,3\\,(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(7)\\,-\\,(-2)\\,(-10)\\,+\\,3\\,(9) \\\\  & =-7-20+27 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
