    "q-317313": {
      "dom_id": "step-q317313",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Let $a=⟨1,-3,0⟩$, $b=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $c=⟨2,0,-4⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(2\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 0)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,((-2)\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,0\\,((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1\\,(-8)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,(8)\\,+\\,0\\,(-4) \\\\  & =-8+24+0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
